## 17.3 Concept

### 17.3.1 The frame — what is actually on the wire

This section was missing from v5.3. The rest of §17.3 refers to the identifier, the ACK slot and the DLC by name, so here is the layout they live in. Standard frame, 11-bit identifier, data frame:

```text
 bits:   1        11        1     1    1     4        0..64        15    1   1    1      7
       +----+------------+-----+----+----+------+--------------+-------+---+---+----+---------+
       |SOF | identifier | RTR |IDE | r0 | DLC  |     data     |  CRC  |del|ACK|del|   EOF   |
       +----+------------+-----+----+----+------+--------------+-------+---+---+----+---------+
        |    \__________________/         \____________________/
        |     arbitration field            payload, DLC bytes
        |     lower ID wins the bus        0 to 8 bytes
        |
        dominant bit, wakes every node
```

| Field | Bits | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| **SOF** | 1 | Start of frame. A single dominant bit; every node hard-synchronizes on its falling edge. |
| **Identifier** | 11 | **The message name, not a destination.** Also the priority — see §17.3.2. |
| **RTR** | 1 | Remote transmission request. `0` = data frame, `1` = "somebody please send this ID." |
| **IDE** | 1 | Identifier extension. `0` = standard 11-bit, `1` = extended 29-bit. This is `.extd` in `twai_message_t`. |
| **r0** | 1 | Reserved, transmitted dominant. |
| **DLC** | 4 | Data length code, the payload byte count, 0 to 8. This is `.data_length_code`. |
| **Data** | 0–64 | The payload. DLC × 8 bits. |
| **CRC** | 15 (+1 delimiter) | Computed over everything from SOF through the data field. |
| **ACK** | 1 (+1 delimiter) | Transmitter sends recessive; **any** receiver that got a good CRC overwrites it dominant. See §17.3.3. |
| **EOF** | 7 | Seven recessive bits ending the frame. |

**Extended frames** replace the 11-bit ID with 29 bits and insert **SRR** and **IDE** between the two ID halves. `.extd = 1` in `twai_message_t` selects this. Everything else in the lab is unchanged.

> **[KEY POINT] The one line to carry into an interview**
>
> **There is no receiver address anywhere in the frame.** The identifier names the *message*. Every node hears every frame and decides — in hardware, via the acceptance filter in §17.6 — whether it cares. That is the single biggest structural difference from I²C, where the first byte is a device address.

### 17.3.2 Arbitration — how the ID becomes the priority

The bus has two states. **Recessive** is idle, both lines resting near 2.5 V, differential ≈ 0 V, logic **1**. **Dominant** is driven, differential ≈ 2 V, logic **0**. A dominant bit from any node overrides a recessive bit from every other node. This is the same open-drain, wired-AND arrangement as I²C.

```text
 3.5 V ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─┌──────────┐─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ┌──────────┐─ ─ ─   CAN_H
                    │          │              │          │
 2.5 V ─────────────┘          └──────────────┘          └──────  idle level
                    ┐          ┌              ┐          ┌
 1.5 V ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─└──────────┘─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ └──────────┘─ ─ ─   CAN_L

 state:  recessive   dominant    recessive     dominant   recessive
 logic:      1           0           1             0          1
```

Now, as nodes transmit their identifiers bit by bit, **each one also listens to the bus**. A node that transmits a recessive 1 but hears a dominant 0 knows another node with a lower ID is transmitting, so it immediately stops and becomes a receiver.

The result: the lower ID wins, **the winner's transmission is completely unaffected** — it does not even know a contest happened — and the loser retries automatically as soon as the bus is idle. No data is corrupted and no bandwidth is wasted.

Compare Ethernet's classic approach, where a collision is detected only *after* both messages are already ruined, and both sides then wait a random interval and retry. CAN's scheme resolves contention before any damage occurs, which is what allows you to reason about worst-case latency — the thing a real-time control bus actually needs.

**So the ID is the priority.** That is a design decision you make when you lay out a system: safety-critical messages get low IDs so nothing can delay them.

### 17.3.3 Acknowledgement, and why a lone board needs NO_ACK

Every CAN frame must be **acknowledged by at least one other node**. Receivers assert a dominant bit in the ACK slot to confirm they heard it, which gives the transmitter genuine feedback that somebody is listening — something I²C also provides per byte and SPI provides not at all.

It also means a single board with nothing else on the bus will report errors indefinitely, because nobody acknowledges it. After enough consecutive errors the controller takes itself off the bus entirely, a state called **bus-off** — the protocol deliberately removing a node it believes is faulty.

`TWAI_MODE_NO_ACK` waives the acknowledgement requirement so one board can test itself. That is why Part A works, and understanding *why the mode exists* is worth more than the mode itself.

### 17.3.4 Frames are small on purpose

A CAN frame carries **0 to 8 bytes**. That looks stingy until you consider what it buys: short frames mean short, *bounded* transmission times, which is what lets you calculate the worst-case delay for a high-priority message on a loaded bus. A protocol that allowed large payloads would let one long frame delay a brake message unpredictably.

CAN is a real-time control bus, not a file-transfer link, and every design choice in it trades throughput for predictability. **CAN FD** is the modern extension that allows 64-byte payloads and a faster data phase for applications that need more, and it is worth knowing by name.

## 17.4 Bit timing — the section v5.3 was missing

`app_main` contains one line that v5.3 never explained:

```c
twai_timing_config_t t = TWAI_TIMING_CONFIG_500KBITS();
```

That macro hides the entire bit-timing model. It works in Part A because a node in loopback only has to agree with itself. **On a real two-node bus it is the first thing that breaks**, and it is the question that follows "explain CAN arbitration" in an interview.

### 17.4.1 One bit is divided into time quanta

```text
        |<------------------ one nominal bit time ------------------>|
        +-------+---------------+-----------------+-----------------+
        | SYNC  |   PROP_SEG    |   PHASE_SEG1    |   PHASE_SEG2    |
        | 1 Tq  |    1-8 Tq     |     1-8 Tq      |     1-8 Tq      |
        +-------+---------------+-----------------+-----------------+
                                                  ^
                                            SAMPLE POINT
                                       the bus is read exactly here
```

| Segment | Purpose |
|---|---|
| **SYNC** | Always 1 Tq. The edge is expected to fall here. Hard synchronization on SOF happens against this segment. |
| **PROP_SEG** | Covers physical round-trip delay: cable, both transceivers, and back. This is what limits bus length — see §17.4.4. |
| **PHASE_SEG1 / PHASE_SEG2** | Absorb oscillator drift between nodes. Resynchronization lengthens SEG1 or shortens SEG2 by up to **SJW** quanta. |

The **sample point** sits at the SEG1/SEG2 boundary, conventionally **75% to 87.5%** through the bit. Every node on the bus must sample at close to the same place, because arbitration requires two nodes to agree, bit by bit, about what is on the wire at that instant.

### 17.4.2 The arithmetic

One time quantum is the peripheral clock divided by the prescaler:

```text
    Tq        = brp / f_src

    bit time  = (1 + tseg_1 + tseg_2) x Tq          <- the 1 is SYNC

    bit rate  = 1 / bit time
                = f_src / (brp x (1 + tseg_1 + tseg_2))

    sample point = (1 + tseg_1) / (1 + tseg_1 + tseg_2)
```

On the ESP32-S3 the TWAI source clock is **APB at 80 MHz**.

**[DERIVED]** For `TWAI_TIMING_CONFIG_500KBITS()` with `brp = 8`, `tseg_1 = 15`, `tseg_2 = 4`:

```text
    Tq           = 8 / 80 MHz          = 100 ns
    total quanta = 1 + 15 + 4          = 20 Tq
    bit time     = 20 x 100 ns         = 2.0 us
    bit rate     = 1 / 2.0 us          = 500 kbit/s        <- checks out
    sample point = 16 / 20             = 80%
```

**[DERIVED]** For `TWAI_TIMING_CONFIG_125KBITS()` with `brp = 32` and the same segments:

```text
    Tq           = 32 / 80 MHz         = 400 ns
    bit time     = 20 x 400 ns         = 8.0 us
    bit rate     = 125 kbit/s
    sample point = 80%                 <- unchanged; only the prescaler moved
```

> **[NOTE] Confirm the segment values against your own header**
>
> The `brp` and `tseg` numbers above are the ESP-IDF v5.5 values for the ESP32-S3 and are quoted so you can follow the arithmetic. Confirm them yourself rather than trusting the page:
>
> ```text
> Select-String -Path "$env:IDF_PATH\components\hal\include\hal\twai_types.h" -Pattern "TIMING_CONFIG_500KBITS" -Context 0,3
> ```
>
> The **2.0 µs bit time is definitional** — it follows from 500 kbit/s regardless of how the segments are split — so proof case 5 stands even if your header divides the quanta differently.

### 17.4.3 Why there is no clock line — bit stuffing

CAN has two wires and neither is a clock. Unlike I²C's SCL, receivers recover timing from the **edges in the data itself**, resynchronizing on every recessive-to-dominant transition.

A long run of identical bits has no edges, and the receivers would drift. So after **five consecutive identical bits the transmitter inserts one bit of the opposite polarity**, which receivers remove. This is **bit stuffing**, and it applies from SOF through the CRC field.

Two consequences worth knowing:

- **Frame length is not fixed.** A worst-case standard data frame with 8 bytes runs about 130 bits on the wire rather than the 111 the field table sums to. Your worst-case latency math has to use the stuffed length.
- **Six identical bits in a row is illegal**, which is exactly how an error frame is signalled: a node that detects a problem deliberately transmits six dominant bits, violating the rule on purpose so every other node sees it immediately.

### 17.4.4 Bit rate and bus length are coupled

PROP_SEG must cover the round trip: a bit must reach the far end of the bus and back within one bit time, because that is what arbitration requires. Signal propagation is roughly 5 ns/m each way in twisted pair, plus transceiver loop delay.

| Bit rate | Bit time | Practical max bus length |
|---|---|---|
| 1 Mbit/s | 1.0 µs | ~40 m |
| 500 kbit/s | 2.0 µs | ~100 m |
| 250 kbit/s | 4.0 µs | ~250 m |
| 125 kbit/s | 8.0 µs | ~500 m |

This is why a vehicle powertrain bus runs at 500 kbit/s and a long industrial run drops to 125. It is not a bandwidth choice, it is a geometry choice.

> **[KEY POINT] The failure mode this section prevents**
>
> Two nodes at different bit rates produce a bus where **nothing is ever acknowledged**. The transmitter's error counter climbs, it goes error-passive, then bus-off — which looks exactly like a wiring fault, a missing terminator, or a dead transceiver.
>
> When you build Part B, check the bit rate on both nodes *before* you check anything physical. It is the cheapest test and the most likely cause.

