## 17.6 Phase 1 — the complete main.c

> **[WARNING] Read this before you flash: NO_ACK is not the same as self-reception**
>
> These are **two separate settings** and the loopback needs both. Espressif's own self-test example uses the No Acknowledge Mode *and* Self Reception Request.
>
> | Setting | Where | What it does |
> |---|---|---|
> | `TWAI_MODE_NO_ACK` | the *driver* config | A transmission counts as successful **without** an ACK from another node. Without this, a lone board goes bus-off in about a second. |
> | `.self = 1` | each *message* | Asks the controller to also **receive its own transmission**. Without this, `twai_transmit()` succeeds and `twai_receive()` times out. |
>
> Set only the mode — which an earlier edition of this lab did — and you get the confusing symptom of `tx_ok` climbing while `rx_ok` stays at zero, with no error anywhere. `.flags = TWAI_MSG_FLAG_SELF` is the older spelling of the same bit and still works.

```c
// ============================================================================
//  Lab 17 -- CAN / TWAI                                            (v5.4)
//
//  Goal:   Send and receive a CAN frame on one board, filter in hardware,
//          watch the error counters, force bus-off and recover from it.
//  Verify: rx_ok tracks tx_ok with the jumper fitted. Changing the TX id to
//          OTHER_ID makes rx_ok stop while tx_ok keeps climbing.
//  Gotcha: NO_ACK mode and .self = 1 are DIFFERENT things. You need both.
//          In the mask, a 1 bit means DON'T CARE -- inverted from intuition.
//
//  Part A: jumper GPIO5 -> GPIO4. No transceiver, no second board.
// ============================================================================
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <inttypes.h>
#include "freertos/FreeRTOS.h"
#include "freertos/task.h"
#include "driver/twai.h"
#include "esp_log.h"

#define TX_PIN      GPIO_NUM_5
#define RX_PIN      GPIO_NUM_4
#define MY_ID       0x123        // the ID this node transmits and accepts
#define OTHER_ID    0x456        // used in proof case 2 to show the filter rejecting

// ---- acceptance filter arithmetic (LEGACY twai_* API, ESP-IDF 5.x) -------
// A single filter with 11-bit standard IDs expects the ID in the TOP 11 bits
// of a 32-bit word, i.e. shifted left by 21:   0x123 -> 0x24600000
//
// In the MASK, a 1 bit means "DON'T CARE". That is inverted from what most
// people assume. The low 21 bits are unused padding below the ID, so they are
// always don't-care.  All-11-ID-bits-zero => every ID bit must match exactly.
//
// NOTE for later: ESP-IDF v6's NEW node API reverses this convention -- there
// a mask '1' means "must match". If you ever port this, re-read the docs.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
#define FILTER_CODE(id)   ((uint32_t)(id) << 21)
#define FILTER_MASK_EXACT 0x001FFFFFu

static const char *TAG = "lab17";

// Counters. g_arb_lost is declared here rather than in the extension, so the
// extension in 17.10.1 is a two-line edit and not a compile error.
static uint32_t g_tx_ok, g_tx_fail, g_rx_ok, g_busoff, g_recovered, g_arb_lost;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- helpers
static const char *state_name(twai_state_t s)
{
    switch (s) {
        case TWAI_STATE_STOPPED:    return "STOPPED";
        case TWAI_STATE_RUNNING:    return "RUNNING";
        case TWAI_STATE_BUS_OFF:    return "BUS_OFF";
        case TWAI_STATE_RECOVERING: return "RECOVERING";
        default:                    return "?";
    }
}

static void log_status(const char *why)
{
    twai_status_info_t st;
    if (twai_get_status_info(&st) != ESP_OK) return;
    ESP_LOGI(TAG, "[%s] state=%s tx_err=%" PRIu32 " rx_err=%" PRIu32
                  " tx_q=%" PRIu32 " rx_q=%" PRIu32,
             why, state_name(st.state),
             st.tx_error_counter, st.rx_error_counter,
             st.msgs_to_tx, st.msgs_to_rx);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- TX task
static void tx_task(void *arg)
{
    (void)arg;
    uint8_t counter = 0;

    for (;;) {
        twai_message_t tx = {
            .identifier       = MY_ID,
            .data_length_code = 4,
            .self             = 1,     // <-- SELF RECEPTION. Without this the
            .data             = { 0xDE, 0xAD, 0xBE, counter },  // rx queue stays empty.
        };
        // .extd stays 0 = standard 11-bit ID;  .rtr 0 = data frame, not a request

        esp_err_t err = twai_transmit(&tx, pdMS_TO_TICKS(1000));
        if (err == ESP_OK) {
            g_tx_ok++;
        } else {
            g_tx_fail++;
            ESP_LOGW(TAG, "tx failed: %s", esp_err_to_name(err));
        }
        counter++;
        vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(500));
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- RX task
static void rx_task(void *arg)
{
    (void)arg;
    for (;;) {
        twai_message_t rx;
        esp_err_t err = twai_receive(&rx, pdMS_TO_TICKS(2000));

        if (err == ESP_OK) {
            g_rx_ok++;
            char buf[3 * 8 + 1] = {0};
            for (int i = 0; i < rx.data_length_code && i < 8; i++)
                sprintf(buf + i * 3, "%02X ", rx.data[i]);
            ESP_LOGI(TAG, "RX id=0x%03" PRIX32 " dlc=%d data=[ %s]",
                     rx.identifier, rx.data_length_code, buf);
        } else if (err == ESP_ERR_TIMEOUT) {
            ESP_LOGW(TAG, "no frame in 2 s");   // real information on a bus
        } else {
            ESP_LOGE(TAG, "rx error: %s", esp_err_to_name(err));
        }
    }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------- alert / recovery
// The task that turns a demo into a node you could ship.
static void alert_task(void *arg)
{
    (void)arg;
    for (;;) {
        uint32_t alerts;
        if (twai_read_alerts(&alerts, pdMS_TO_TICKS(1000)) != ESP_OK) continue;

        if (alerts & TWAI_ALERT_ERR_PASS)
            ESP_LOGW(TAG, "ERROR PASSIVE -- an error counter passed 127");
        if (alerts & TWAI_ALERT_BUS_ERROR)
            ESP_LOGW(TAG, "bus error detected");
        if (alerts & TWAI_ALERT_TX_FAILED)
            ESP_LOGW(TAG, "a transmission failed after retries");
        if (alerts & TWAI_ALERT_RX_QUEUE_FULL)
            ESP_LOGW(TAG, "rx queue full -- frames were dropped");

        if (alerts & TWAI_ALERT_BUS_OFF) {
            g_busoff++;
            ESP_LOGE(TAG, "BUS-OFF. the node removed itself from the bus");
            log_status("bus-off");
            // Recovery MUST be initiated from task context, never an ISR.
            // The controller then waits for 128 occurrences of 11 recessive
            // bits before rejoining -- that is the CAN standard, not IDF.
            vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(100));
            esp_err_t e = twai_initiate_recovery();
            if (e != ESP_OK) ESP_LOGE(TAG, "recovery start failed: %s",
                                      esp_err_to_name(e));
        }

        if (alerts & TWAI_ALERT_BUS_RECOVERED) {
            g_recovered++;
            ESP_LOGW(TAG, "recovered. restarting the driver");
            // Recovery leaves the driver STOPPED, not RUNNING. Miss this and
            // the node sits silent forever, looking like it never recovered.
            esp_err_t e = twai_start();
            if (e != ESP_OK) ESP_LOGE(TAG, "restart failed: %s",
                                      esp_err_to_name(e));
            log_status("recovered");
        }
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- stats
static void stats_task(void *arg)
{
    (void)arg;
    for (;;) {
        vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(5000));
        ESP_LOGW(TAG, "tx_ok=%" PRIu32 " tx_fail=%" PRIu32 " rx_ok=%" PRIu32
                      " bus_off=%" PRIu32 " recovered=%" PRIu32,
                 g_tx_ok, g_tx_fail, g_rx_ok, g_busoff, g_recovered);
        log_status("periodic");
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- app_main
void app_main(void)
{
    twai_general_config_t g =
        TWAI_GENERAL_CONFIG_DEFAULT(TX_PIN, RX_PIN, TWAI_MODE_NO_ACK);
    g.alerts_enabled = TWAI_ALERT_ERR_PASS | TWAI_ALERT_BUS_ERROR |
                       TWAI_ALERT_TX_FAILED | TWAI_ALERT_RX_QUEUE_FULL |
                       TWAI_ALERT_BUS_OFF  | TWAI_ALERT_BUS_RECOVERED;
    g.rx_queue_len = 10;
    g.tx_queue_len = 10;

    // 500 kbit/s = 20 Tq of 100 ns, sample point at 80%. See 17.4.
    // Proof case 5 swaps this for TWAI_TIMING_CONFIG_125KBITS().
    twai_timing_config_t t = TWAI_TIMING_CONFIG_500KBITS();

    twai_filter_config_t f = {
        .acceptance_code = FILTER_CODE(MY_ID),
        .acceptance_mask = FILTER_MASK_EXACT,
        .single_filter   = true,
    };

    ESP_ERROR_CHECK(twai_driver_install(&g, &t, &f));
    ESP_ERROR_CHECK(twai_start());
    ESP_LOGI(TAG, "TWAI up at 500 kbit/s, accepting only 0x%03X "
                  "(code 0x%08" PRIX32 ", mask 0x%08" PRIX32 ")",
             MY_ID, FILTER_CODE(MY_ID), (uint32_t)FILTER_MASK_EXACT);
    log_status("start");

    // Check every task creation. A silent failure here shows up later as a
    // mystery -- no frames received, or no alerts handled, with no clue why.
    BaseType_t ok = pdPASS;
    ok &= xTaskCreate(rx_task,    "rx",    4096, NULL, 6, NULL);
    ok &= xTaskCreate(alert_task, "alert", 4096, NULL, 7, NULL);  // above rx
    ok &= xTaskCreate(tx_task,    "tx",    4096, NULL, 5, NULL);
    ok &= xTaskCreate(stats_task, "stats", 4096, NULL, 3, NULL);
    if (ok != pdPASS) {
        ESP_LOGE(TAG, "task creation failed -- out of heap?");
        abort();
    }
}
```

## 17.7 Code walkthrough, line by line

**.self = 1** · inside the transmit message

The single line an earlier edition of this lab omitted, and the reason it could not work.

`twai_message_t` holds a union of bitfields and a `flags` word, so `.self = 1` and the older `.flags = TWAI_MSG_FLAG_SELF` set the same bit. It asks the controller to place a copy of the transmitted frame into its own receive queue.

**Why the mode alone is not enough:** `TWAI_MODE_NO_ACK` changes what counts as a *successful transmission*. Self-reception changes what gets *received*. Two different questions, two different settings. With the jumper fitted but no `.self`, the controller does see its own bits on RX — but a CAN controller normally suppresses reception of a frame it transmitted itself, so the queue stays empty.

**#define FILTER_CODE(id) ((uint32_t)(id) << 21)**

The bit arithmetic most people get wrong. For a **single filter with 11-bit standard IDs**, the hardware expects the ID in the **top 11 bits** of a 32-bit word, so you shift left by 21. Concretely, `0x123` becomes `0x24600000`.

Get this wrong and the filter appears to work in Part A — because in loopback you only ever hear yourself — and then silently rejects everything on a real bus. Worth checking with a calculator once.

**#define FILTER_MASK_EXACT 0x001FFFFF**

**In the mask, a 1 bit means "don't care."** That is inverted from what most people assume, and it is the second half of the same trap.

The low 21 bits are the unused padding below the ID, so they are always don't-care. Setting the top 11 bits to 0 means "every one of these must match." Result: exactly one ID accepted.

To accept a *range*, set the bits that vary. `0x120` through `0x12F` differ only in the bottom four ID bits, so `code = 0x120 << 21` and `mask = (0x00F << 21) | 0x1FFFFF`.

**twai_timing_config_t t = TWAI_TIMING_CONFIG_500KBITS()**

The line v5.3 left unexplained. It expands to a prescaler plus segment counts that divide the 80 MHz APB clock into 20 time quanta of 100 ns, giving a 2.0 µs bit and a sample point at 80%. §17.4 has the derivation; proof case 5 measures it.

The reason it matters: **this is the one setting every node on a bus must agree on.** The filter, the mode, and the queue depths are local decisions. Bit timing is a contract.

**g.alerts_enabled = ...**

The line that turns a demo into a node. Alerts are the driver's way of telling you about conditions you would otherwise never see: error-passive, bus errors, failed transmissions, a full receive queue, bus-off, and recovery.

**You must opt in.** A driver installed with the default config raises none of these, which is why the earlier edition of this lab could not detect bus-off — it never asked to be told.

**g.rx_queue_len = 10**

How many received frames the driver buffers before dropping. The default is small. On a bus at 500 kbit/s a burst arrives far faster than a task can drain it, so this is your absorption buffer — the same reasoning as the DMA pool depth in Lab 20. `TWAI_ALERT_RX_QUEUE_FULL` tells you when it was not enough.

**twai_get_status_info(&st)**

The single most useful diagnostic call in the API. It reports the controller **state** and, critically, the two **error counters**.

CAN nodes police themselves with those counters. Every transmit or receive error increments them and every success decrements them. Cross 127 and the node becomes **error passive** — still talking, but no longer allowed to assert dominant error flags. Cross 255 and it goes **bus-off** and removes itself entirely.

That is the protocol deliberately ejecting a node it believes is faulty, so one broken device cannot take down a vehicle bus. Watching those counters climb is the clearest window into bus health you get.

**the alert_task, and why recovery lives here**

`twai_read_alerts()` blocks until something happens, so this task costs nothing while the bus is healthy.

**`twai_initiate_recovery()` must be called from task context, never from an ISR.** After it is called, the CAN standard requires the controller to observe **128 occurrences of 11 consecutive recessive bits** before rejoining — a mandatory cooling-off period, not an ESP-IDF invention. At 500 kbit/s that is a few milliseconds, and during it the node is deaf.

**ESP_ERROR_CHECK(twai_start()) after TWAI_ALERT_BUS_RECOVERED**

The step people miss. **Recovery leaves the driver in the STOPPED state, not RUNNING.** If you do not restart it, the node completes recovery and then sits there silently forever, which looks exactly like a node that never recovered at all.

**alert_task at priority 7, above rx_task at 6**

Deliberate. When the bus is failing, handling the failure matters more than draining frames. If `rx_task` outranked the alert task, a flood of errors could keep you busy receiving while bus-off went unhandled.

**esp_err_to_name(err)**

Turns an `esp_err_t` into a readable string. Cheap, and it converts `rx error: 0x107` into `rx error: ESP_ERR_TIMEOUT`. Use it in every error log you write.

**%03" PRIX32 " for the identifier**

`twai_message_t.identifier` is `uint32_t`, whose underlying type varies across ESP-IDF targets and versions, so `%03lX` is not portable and produces a warning on some builds. `PRIX32` from `<inttypes.h>` expands to whatever is correct. Three hex digits is exactly right for an 11-bit ID (max `0x7FF`).

