```

> **[KEY POINT] Expected output, Part A with the GPIO5–GPIO4 jumper fitted**
>
> **Corrected in v5.4.** The v5.3 manual printed `filtering for 0x123`, which is not what the code in §17.6 logs. The startup line includes the computed code and mask, and seeing `0x24600000` there is your confirmation that the shift-by-21 arithmetic is right before you debug anything else.

```text
I lab17: TWAI up at 500 kbit/s, accepting only 0x123 (code 0x24600000, mask 0x001FFFFF)
I lab17: [start] state=RUNNING tx_err=0 rx_err=0 tx_q=0 rx_q=0
I lab17: RX id=0x123 dlc=4 data=[ DE AD BE 00 ]
I lab17: RX id=0x123 dlc=4 data=[ DE AD BE 01 ]
I lab17: RX id=0x123 dlc=4 data=[ DE AD BE 02 ]
W lab17: tx_ok=10 tx_fail=0 rx_ok=10 bus_off=0 recovered=0
I lab17: [periodic] state=RUNNING tx_err=0 rx_err=0 tx_q=0 rx_q=0
```

The counter byte incrementing proves these are fresh frames, not one frame echoing.

> **[WARNING] If you see nothing received**
>
> **Jumper missing or on the wrong pins.** GPIO5 to GPIO4, both on the expansion base left column.
>
> **Filter wrong.** Check the startup line first — if `code` is not `0x24600000` the arithmetic is wrong, not the wiring. Then temporarily swap in `TWAI_FILTER_CONFIG_ACCEPT_ALL()`. If frames appear, it is the filter.
>
> **Wrong mode.** Without `TWAI_MODE_NO_ACK`, a lone node gets no acknowledgement, error counters climb, and it goes bus-off within a second or two. You would see that in the log now, which is the point of having the alert task.

## 17.9 Phase 3 — prove it

| # | Do this | Expected |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Run as printed with the jumper. | `rx_ok` tracks `tx_ok`, state stays `RUNNING`, both error counters 0. |
| 2 | **Prove the filter rejects.** Change `.identifier` in `tx_task` to `OTHER_ID` and reflash. | `tx_ok` still climbs, **`rx_ok` stays 0**, and you get "no frame in 2 s". The hardware dropped every frame without waking the CPU. Change it back. |
| 3 | **Force bus-off.** Change the mode to `TWAI_MODE_NORMAL` with the jumper still fitted and no transceiver. | Error counters climb, `ERROR PASSIVE`, then **`BUS-OFF`**, then recovery and a restart. `bus_off` and `recovered` both increment. |
| 4 | Leave case 3 running for two minutes. | It cycles: bus-off, recover, restart, bus-off again. **The node never gets permanently stuck** — that is the property you are demonstrating. |
| 5 | **Measure the bit time.** New in v5.4 — see below. | 2.0 µs at 500 kbit/s, 8.0 µs at 125 kbit/s. |

> **[KEY POINT] Case 3 is the one to record in your debug log**
>
> Nothing external is needed, and "I forced a node into bus-off and watched it recover" is a complete answer to a fault-handling question.

### Proof case 5 — bit timing on the logic analyzer

This closes the gap §17.4 opened, needs only hardware you already own, and produces a **[MEASURE]** number rather than a quoted one.

1. Restore the code to Part A as printed (mode `NO_ACK`, ID `MY_ID`).
2. Clip the FX2LP to **GPIO5** and ground. TX is push-pull 3.3 V logic, so no transceiver is needed to see the bit stream.
3. In PulseView set the sample rate to **12 MHz**. At 500 kbit/s that is 24 samples per bit, which is enough to place edges cleanly. 4 MHz gives only 8 and the measurement gets noisy.
4. Capture, then zoom into the SOF falling edge at the start of a frame.
5. Measure the narrowest single-bit pulse you can find. **[MEASURE]** Record it.

**[DERIVED]** It should be **2.0 µs**. Now:

6. Change `app_main` to `TWAI_TIMING_CONFIG_125KBITS()`, rebuild, reflash, recapture.
7. **[MEASURE]** Record the new narrowest pulse.

**[DERIVED]** It should be **8.0 µs** — exactly 4× longer, because only the prescaler changed and the quanta count did not.

> **[KEY POINT] Why this is the better debug-log entry**
>
> Case 3 shows you can handle a fault. Case 5 shows you predicted a number from a datasheet formula and then confirmed it on an instrument. That second thing is much rarer in a student, and it is the shape of answer that ends a "have you really used CAN?" question.
>
> While you are in PulseView, run the **sigrok CAN decoder** on the same capture. It will annotate SOF, the identifier, DLC, data bytes, CRC and ACK — the frame from §17.3.1, on your own bench, from your own board. Screenshot it.

## 17.10 Extensions, worked

### 17.10.1 — Arbitration, counted

With two boards and two transceivers, the honest single-board-instrumented version is to count **arbitration-lost alerts**, which the controller reports directly.

`g_arb_lost` is already declared in §17.6, so this is a two-line edit:

```c
    // add to the alerts enabled in app_main:
    g.alerts_enabled |= TWAI_ALERT_ARB_LOST;

    // and in alert_task:
    if (alerts & TWAI_ALERT_ARB_LOST) {
        g_arb_lost++;
        // no logging here in a tight loop -- just count it
    }
```

To see it in the periodic line, extend `stats_task`:

```c
        ESP_LOGW(TAG, "tx_ok=%" PRIu32 " tx_fail=%" PRIu32 " rx_ok=%" PRIu32
                      " bus_off=%" PRIu32 " recovered=%" PRIu32
                      " arb_lost=%" PRIu32,
                 g_tx_ok, g_tx_fail, g_rx_ok, g_busoff, g_recovered,
                 g_arb_lost);
```

**[MEASURE]** Run two boards transmitting continuously, one with ID `0x080` and one with `0x600`, both in `TWAI_MODE_NORMAL` on a properly terminated bus **at the same bit rate** (§17.4 — check this first). Record `g_arb_lost` on each after 60 s.

**[DERIVED]** The expectation: the `0x600` node accumulates arbitration losses and the `0x080` node accumulates approximately none, because `0x080` is numerically lower and its dominant bits override. Neither node loses a *frame* — the loser retries — but only one of them is ever delayed.

**If you do not have two transceivers**, do not fake it. Write the reasoning and the intended method in your debug log, mark it "not run — no second node," and move on. Proof cases 1 through 5 in §17.9 already carry this lab, and an honest "not measured" is worth more than an invented number.

### 17.10.2 — Worst-case latency from the frame layout

A paper exercise that uses §17.3.1 and §17.4 together, and it is a common interview follow-up.

**[DERIVED]** A standard data frame with 8 bytes sums to 111 bits from the field table. Bit stuffing (§17.4.3) can add up to about 19 more in the worst case, so call it **130 bits**. At 500 kbit/s with a 2.0 µs bit:

```text
    worst-case frame time = 130 x 2.0 us = 260 us
```

So a high-priority message that arrives just after a low-priority frame has started waits at most one frame time — about **260 µs** — before it can begin arbitrating, and once it arbitrates it wins. That bounded number is the entire reason CAN is used for brakes.

Contrast with the same question on Ethernet, where a collision means both frames are destroyed and both sides back off a *random* interval. There is no bound.

